export abstract anchor FactoryBackground:
    illustrativeModels:
        The user's main-agent example is Claude Opus 5.5, mid-tier example is
        Sonnet, and cheap implementation-agent example is DeepSeek Flash. These
        illustrate the intended intelligence and cost hierarchy; they are not
        verified model identifiers or fixed model requirements.
    proposedFoundation:
        DeepSeek Harness is a candidate foundation and is explicitly not
        required. Its official repository is
        https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness. Repository documentation
        describes a plugin-based harness in developer preview. Suitability for
        this factory's orchestration and delegation remains to be evaluated.
    candidateTokenOptimization:
        The engineer is interested in evaluating Jev from TypeSafe AI to reduce
        token usage. Adoption is not decided. Official documentation at
        https://docs.typesafe.ai/introduction/quickstart and the vendor site at
        https://typesafe.ai describe structured decisions with confidence
        estimates. Candidate uses include model-tier routing and context
        relevance scoring; actual savings and decision quality require
        evaluation. These uses are suggestions, not confirmed requirements.
    existingPrototype:
        The engineer already has an ad hoc factory described through a running
        Claude prompt for the Popcorn project at
        /home/hyphyphyph/matter-and-logic/popcorn. Related orchestration artifacts
        live in /home/hyphyphyph/matter-and-logic/popcorn-build.
        Inspected reference documents include popcorn/progress/factory-handoff.md,
        popcorn/factory-dashboard/README.md, popcorn-build/ORCHESTRATOR-HANDOFF.md,
        and popcorn-build/RUNBOOK.md. They document dispatch, planning, merge
        workers, usage monitoring, recovery procedures, a dashboard, and a Jev
        progress service. These are observations about the existing prototype,
        not automatically requirements for the general-purpose factory. The
        running Claude prompt itself has not been inspected.
    initialSuccessCriteria:
        The new factory should be able to resume the engineer's existing
        Popcorn work from its current progress. Popcorn's workflow and operating
        rules must remain project-specific rather than being baked into the
        general-purpose factory. Detailed acceptance scenarios and the exact
        boundary of work to resume remain unresolved.
    openQuestions:
        - Exact code-module wire schema, settings request mapping, and response extraction.
        - Retention close and submission-resolution events.
        - How conformance independence is enforced and audited.
        - Feasibility of attempt-token injection and discovery per harness.
        - Hard per-attempt ceilings and residual budget exposure where providers do not enforce limits.
        - Canonical payload fingerprints and idempotency claim-lease defaults.
        - Exact brief and handoff schema.
        - Exact required behavior and minimum output for onboard, start, submit, answer, and shutdown.
        - Dashboard presentation and its authentication model.
        - Whether a dedicated drift report is ever needed beyond agent reconciliation after specification updates.
        - Configuration of intelligence levels and roles.
        - Engineer-set model-routing thresholds, evidence thresholds for adding a category, and reviewer-based model evaluation methods.
        - Detailed responsibilities and handoffs for merging, testing, and review agents.
        - Integration ordering and merge strategy for concurrent worktree results.
        - Recovery reconciliation details for in-flight external effects.
        - Account allowance visibility, concrete provider conversion tables, burn-rate values, and exposure bounds.
        - PID-style resource control measurements, outputs, tuning, and speed-quality-economy tradeoffs.
        - Per-harness signal availability and adapter implementation details.
        - DeepSeek Harness integration and support for the chosen agent providers.
        - Mapping existing Popcorn artifacts and progress into the new factory, the detailed resumption mapping, and additional acceptance scenarios.
        - Existing Jev progress integration and its relevance to proposed token optimization.
        - Implementation-specific update and restart criteria, review cadence values, and routing evidence thresholds.
        - Whether and how model-performance learning transfers between project-specific factories.
        - The role, scope, technology, and relationship of the reference implementation.
        - Broker, credential lease, and provider-specific secret handling.
    specificationStatus:
        Twelve interview rounds totaling 115 answered questions are complete and
        incorporated. Round 12 was an autonomous implementability round of 20
        questions, each answered here and challenged by two review rounds. No
        interview round is in progress. The specification awaits the engineer's
        decision whether it is good enough or another round should continue.
        Remaining open questions are recorded explicitly rather than silently
        assumed.
    interviewFocus:
        No round is in progress. Round 12 added lifecycle and attempt state
        machines, cancellation policy, question and changed-answer semantics,
        stall detection, a liveness and discovery contract, retries, priority,
        build and test jobs, budget reservation, persistence and idempotency,
        wiring and validation checks, evidence retention, the descriptor schema,
        scaffold installation and validation, conformance scenarios, and the
        standard operation envelope, actor classes, and core records.
